<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A retailer sold through physical stores, its own website and several marketplaces. Stock levels lived in a warehouse system and store systems, orders in an order management database, and each channel received a stock file once a night. During the day, the same last unit could be sold in a store, online and on a marketplace before anyone knew.</p>
<p>The workarounds made things worse: large safety buffers that hid sellable stock, manual cancellations, and ad hoc point-to-point integrations that each team had built to get fresher data for its own channel.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Every committed change is captured</strong>, without polling or adding load to operational databases.</li>
<li><strong>No dual writes</strong>: an order and its event either both happen or neither does.</li>
<li><strong>Channels read a projection</strong>, not the source systems.</li>
<li><strong>History is replayable</strong>, so a bug in a projection can be fixed and rebuilt.</li>
<li><strong>Contracts are explicit</strong>, so source teams can change their schemas without breaking consumers.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<p>Log-based change data capture reads committed row-level changes from the database's transaction log, so applications consume changes rather than querying the source<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. That became the backbone, with a stream processing layer building inventory views for each consumer.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Sources</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Order management<em>relational DB</em></span><span>Warehouse system<em>stock movements</em></span><span>Store systems<em>sales, returns</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Capture</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Outbox tables<em>same transaction</em></span><span>Debezium<em>Kafka Connect</em></span><span>Schema registry<em>versioned contracts</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Stream</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Kafka topics<em>keyed by SKU and location</em></span><span>Compacted state topics<em>latest per key</em></span><span>Dead-letter topics<em>with reasons</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Project</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Available-to-sell<em>stream processor</em></span><span>Reservations<em>pending orders</em></span><span>Location rollups<em>store, region, online</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Serve</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Redis read model<em>low-latency lookups</em></span><span>Web and app<em>live stock</em></span><span>Marketplace feeds<em>incremental</em></span><span>Analytics<em>same events</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Layered architecture: capture once from the log, project many times for each consumer.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Commit</b><span>Order or stock change and its outbox event written in one database transaction</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Capture</b><span>Debezium reads the log and routes outbox events to domain topics</span></li>
<li><b>Validate</b><span>Schema checked against the registry; malformed events to dead-letter</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Project</b><span>Stream processor updates available-to-sell per SKU and location</span></li>
<li><b>Serve</b><span>Read model updated; channels query it or receive incremental updates</span></li>
<li><b>Reconcile</b><span>Periodic comparison with source counts; drift raised, not hidden</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Every step is idempotent and keyed, so replays and retries do not double-count stock.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>More on the capture side in <a href="/insights/change-data-capture-event-streams/">change data capture and event streams</a>, and on duplicate-safe consumers in <a href="/insights/idempotent-payments-order-pipelines/">idempotent order pipelines</a>.</p>

<h2>The hard part: events that mean something</h2>
<p>Streaming raw table changes was quick to demonstrate and the wrong long-term contract. Consumers ended up decoding internal columns and status codes, and any schema change in the source broke them. The transactional outbox pattern addresses this: the service writes a domain event to an outbox table in the same transaction as the business change, and a relay publishes it<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Debezium's outbox event router does that relay from the log, routing events to topics by aggregate type and keying them by aggregate identifier<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Events were designed with the consuming teams: "stock adjusted", "order allocated", "reservation released", not "row updated".</li>
<li>Keys were chosen for ordering: everything about one SKU at one location lands in one partition, in order.</li>
<li>Contracts went into the schema registry with compatibility rules, following a <a href="/insights/data-contracts-playbook/">data contracts</a> approach.</li>
</ul>
<p>For current state, compacted topics keep the latest record per key and support rebuilding state after a failure<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>, which let new projections bootstrap without a bulk extract from the source.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>CDC moves data quickly; it does not decide what the data means. Design the events with the teams who will read them.</p></div>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Source teams</small><ol><li>Add outbox writes</li><li class="hl">Own event schemas</li><li>Keep compatibility</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Platform</small><ol><li>Run Connect and Kafka</li><li>Operate the registry</li><li>Monitor lag and drift</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Channel teams</small><ol><li>Read projections</li><li class="hl">Report mismatches</li><li>Retire old file feeds</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Producers own their events, the platform owns the pipes, consumers own their projections.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Map</b><ul><li>Stock and order flows</li><li>Where overselling occurs</li><li>Event catalogue draft</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Thin slice</b><ul><li>One source with outbox</li><li>One projection</li><li>One channel in shadow</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Extend</b><ul><li>Remaining sources</li><li>Reservations logic</li><li>Reconciliation jobs</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Switch and hand off</b><ul><li>Channels cut over</li><li>Nightly files retired</li><li>Runbooks and on-call</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Each channel ran in shadow against the nightly file before cutting over.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Operationally, the signals that mattered were connector health, consumer lag per projection and the reconciliation drift report. Those went on one dashboard owned by the platform team, with alerts tied to what a channel would notice (stale stock) rather than to every broker metric. Traces carried the event identifier from commit to read model, so a disputed stock figure could be followed back to the change that produced it.</p>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Log-based CDC</td><td>Polling or nightly extracts</td><td>Captures every committed change with little source load</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transactional outbox</td><td>Raw table change events</td><td>Stable domain contracts; no dual writes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Key by SKU and location</td><td>Key by order</td><td>Ordered updates for the thing being counted</td></tr>
<tr><td>Separate read model</td><td>Channels query sources</td><td>Protects operational systems; fast reads</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reconciliation jobs</td><td>Trust the stream</td><td>Drift is surfaced and explained, not discovered by customers</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: a thin slice end to end before scaling, shadow running against the old feed, and reconciliation from the start. Differently: agree the reservation and allocation rules with operations earlier. Live data exposed business rules that had never been written down, and those took longer to settle than the streaming platform.</p>
<p>If you are planning a similar move off batch, the <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a> page describes how I work with teams on it, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
